The sample mean \(\bar{X}\) is approximately normally distributed due to the Central Limit Theorem, with mean \(\mu_{\bar{X}} = 3\) and standard deviation \(\sigma_{\bar{X}} = 0.5\).

The probability \(P(2.5 \leq \bar{X} \leq 4)\) is found by standardizing to a standard normal variable \(Z\):

- \(z_{\text{lower}} = \frac{2.5 - 3}{0.5} = -1\)
- \(z_{\text{upper}} = \frac{4 - 3}{0.5} = 2\)

Thus, \(P(-1 \leq Z \leq 2) = P(Z \leq 2) - P(Z \leq -1)\).

From standard normal tables:
- \(P(Z \leq 2) = 0.9772\)
- \(P(Z \leq -1) = 0.1587\)

So, \(P(-1 \leq Z \leq 2) = 0.9772 - 0.1587 = 0.8185\).

This matches option I.

