The limit of \(g(x)/x\) as \(x\) approaches infinity is found by evaluating \(g(x)\) explicitly. The function \(g(x)\) is defined as \(g(x) = \lim_{r \to 0} \left( (x+1)^{r+1} - x^{r+1} \right)^{1/r}\). Using L'Hôpital's rule and properties of limits, \(g(x) = (x+1)^{x+1} / x^x\).

Then, \(g(x)/x = (x+1)^{x+1} / x^{x+1} = \left(1 + \frac{1}{x}\right)^{x+1}\). As \(x\) approaches infinity, \(\left(1 + \frac{1}{x}\right)^x \to e\) and \(\left(1 + \frac{1}{x}\right) \to 1\), so the product approaches \(e \cdot 1 = e\).

The value of \(e\) is approximately 2.7182818, which corresponds to option I.

