The heat transfer by radiation between the walls of a thermos bottle depends on the emissivities of the surfaces. For opaque surfaces, emissivity ε is related to reflectance ρ by ε = 1 - ρ. Initially, ρ = 0.9, so ε_initial = 0.1. Finally, ρ = 0.3, so ε_final = 0.7.

For radiation heat transfer between two identical parallel surfaces, the heat transfer rate Q is proportional to 1 / (2/ε - 1). The initial denominator is D_initial = 2/0.1 - 1 = 19, and the final denominator is D_final = 2/0.7 - 1 ≈ 1.857. The factor by which Q increases is Q_final / Q_initial = D_initial / D_final ≈ 19 / 1.857 ≈ 10.23.

The closest option to 10.23 is 10.2.

